WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.260
"...Homeland Security bill is ratified. After eight years, British borders will remain closed.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.080
The deportation of illegal immigrants will continue.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.080
Good morning, our lead story:"

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.940
"The world was stunned today by the death of Diego Ricardo,

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.780
the youngest person on the planet.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:16.280
 

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:19.640
Baby Diego was stabbed outside a bar in Buenos Aires..."

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.080
Look, there we are.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:21.840
 

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.840
The first shot of Alfonso Cuarón's "Children of Men" is a shot of an audience,

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.900
getting information about the state of things.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.360
In the same way that we do.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:32.980
Through the media.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:39.660
You may have missed it, but Cuarón's fundamental motif of contrasting foreground and background

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.940
has already been established, through the media, before this shot even begins.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.200
Let's back up a little.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:54.140
"...The deportation of illegal immigrants will continue. Good morning, our lead story:"

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:59.220
Like everybody else, the media prioritizes information based on its values.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.340
So much attention is paid to the foreground, to the lead story,

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.540
that the background can go by without much notice

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.860
The lead story of "Children of Men" is the story of Theo.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:14.600
In a future world nearing its end because women have lost the ability to have children,

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:20.340
Theo, an apathetic government bureaucrat, returns to his roots as a political activist,

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.000
after he sees that there is still hope left for humanity.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:32.000
The film plays as an exciting action-thriller as Theo helps the pregnant Kee get to safety with the "human project".

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:39.180
But as the plot moves along its linear path, following Theo, the camera repeatedly becomes preoccupied

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.780
with what's going on in the background.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:46.860
And this is a technique that Cuarón has used in the past, and it makes for an interesting effect.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:52.880
As in thousands of other films, we experience the world through the eyes of the main character.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.060
This process of identification is automatic and strong.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:03.820
So strong that we can even be made to sympathize with people who we would otherwise identify as bad or evil.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:10.300
So when the camera actively breaks that identification and shows us imagery the lead character doesnt see

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:11.200
or notice.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:18.420
We're made forcefully aware, in a purely visual way, of the perspective of the film itself.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.160
in "Children of Men", that perspective is absorbed by the background,

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:28.920
by a bleak, grey winter Britain, the only country in the world still under stable government control.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.420
This background, seen always at a sidelong angle,

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:36.000
probes and investigates the conditions of that stability,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:39.640
whether or not it's worth what's sacrificed to have it.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:46.040
Though it's dressed up with a few futuristic flourishes, the world Cuarón presents to us is a reflection

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.040
of our own.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.580
The grim combination of nationalistic, xenophobic politics,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.060
and lipe capitalist consumerism,

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:55.700
isn't something that we have to imagine.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:59.660
"When Mexico sends its people, they're not sending their best.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.800
They're bringing drugs, they're bringing crime, they're rapists..."

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:09.760
In 2006, when this film was made, only five years after 9/11 and at the height of the war on terror,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.380
this was, and in many ways still is, the state of things.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:17.480
"...for thousands of migrants and refugees in the war-torn Middle East and Africa,

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.420
a rude awakening awaits in countries that are moving to close their doors."

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.420
And there's another background, too.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:26.620
A figurative background, of cultural reference.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:33.060
For example, when Kee first reveals her pregnancy, she stands in a posture reminiscent of Botticelli's

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.560
"Birth of Venus",

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.660
one of the many references in this film to maternity.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.260
Jasper and Miriam both recite

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:41.700
"Shantih, shantih, shantih",

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.820
recalling both the Upanishads and T.S. Elliot's

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:45.760
"The Wasteland",

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.560
which is a major source of inspiration for an infertile world that's lost its way.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:57.580
Outside Theo's cousin's Arch of Arts, a pig floats above the old Battersea power station,

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.900
an overt reference to Pink Floyd's album, "Animals"

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:07.100
a concept album loosely based on George Orwell's "Animal Farm", itself a satirical indictment of

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.920
authoritarian Socialism, or Stalinism.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.000
Indeed, there's a lot included in the Arch of Arts scene:

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.140
Here's a Banksy,

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:15.940
here's Michelangelo's "David",

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:21.060
Picasso's "Guernica", significantly framing Theo in the realities of war,

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.060
and, underneath it all, plays King Crimson's progressive rock anthem,

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:27.260
"To the Court of the Crimson King",

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.860
blending the sacred, and the profane.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:36.600
But, Cuarón's referencing becomes more potent, as the intertexts layer and connect.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:41.100
Take this shot here, it utilizes nearly all of the techniques I've mentioned so far.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:46.320
You have the lead story, moving, again, toward its destination at the seed,

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.600
but the camera leaves them for a moment to focus on this mother,

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.360
cradling her dead son.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:57.520
The most immediate citation here is to Michelangelo's famous sculpture, La Pieta,

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.920
which Nigel mentions earlier in the film,

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.460
"...couldn't save La Pieta... Smashed up before we got there."

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:09.440
In that sculpture, Mary holds the crucified Jesus in her arms, questioning the cruelty of men.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:15.260
The image also evokes "Guernica", also shown earlier in the film, a much more modern meditation

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:16.740
on the horrors of war,

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:22.060
and Cuarón has said in interviews that the inspiration for this image came from a photograph out of

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:26.140
the Balkan wars, itself a reference to Michelangelo's work,

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:30.500
whereas the Arch of Arts shows our extracted enplaced in an ivory tower.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:37.560
Cuarón puts art back on the ground, where it belongs, and brings it alive with a number of contexts,

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:43.720
ancient and current, and most importantly, as a background for the main story.

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:51.800
There's an absurd paradox that sits at the center of our modern world. While Capital itself, knows no borders.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:58.460
People and nations struggling to find their identities have sought repeatedly to tighten their borders,

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.000
and restrict peoples from crossing them.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:08.320
What's more, these refugees are increasingly displaced by the militaristic medley of neo-conservative regimes,

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:15.220
and the worsening climate, a result of the unchecked expansion of capitalist enterprises.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:21.000
In "Children of Men", as in our own world, all of these things are intimately connected.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.600
And yet, for each of us individually, it's hard to see anything

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:26.120
but the foreground,

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:27.540
the lead story.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:32.460
That's why the roving, curious preoccupations of Cuarón's camera

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.300
are more than just asides.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:40.140
By providing a disinterested break from identification, they achieve something

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:43.160
we just can't, in our own lives.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:49.060
They let us see the world bare, naked, stripped, for a moment, of subjectivity.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:52.440
This is the radical, political potential of cinema.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:56.700
We can all keep to our main stories, if we want, live in our little worlds.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.580
But sooner or later,

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:00.080
the background

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.200
is going to come for all of us.

